<table border="0" cellpadding="5" style="border-collapse: collapse" width="80%" bordercolor="#BFDFFF">
<tr><td>
			<p style="line-height: 150%" align="justify">&nbsp;</p>
			<table border="1" cellpadding="10" style="border-collapse: collapse" width="100%" bordercolor="#BFDFFF" cellspacing="0">
				<tr>
					<td bgcolor="#FFFFCC">
					<p style="line-height: 150%" align="justify">Why did I 
					create this page? Based on comments that I have received 
					from some people since I retired a few months ago, 
					apparently some 
					people have thought that I <i>had</i> to leave BYU, 
					supposedly because I had hadn't been very productive. They 
					thought this because they had heard that the
					<a href="https://hum.byu.edu/directory/scott-miller">college 
					dean</a> had withdrawn all of my research support -- travel, 
					research assistants, possible professional development 
					leaves, etc. That confusion might be understandable, since 
					the college dean chose not to announce to the college any of 
					my
					<a href="https://news.byu.edu/news/2015-university-conference-awards">
					university-wide</a>
					<a href="https://universityconference.byu.edu/00000173-3595-d39e-a973-fd975e6e0000/uc-awardee-booklet-2017-pdf">
					awards</a>, articles about my research (like the
					<a href="https://magazine.byu.edu/article/big-lang-at-byu/">
					lead article in BYU Magazine</a>), or any of the
					<a href="welcome.asp#grants">$800,000 in grants</a> that I brought to the College of 
					Humanities. Hopefully this page will help to refute the 
					rumors about my supposed <i>lack</i> of productivity. And it 
					is not meant as a criticism of of anyone else in the college 
					(other than the dean and his
					<a href="https://hum.byu.edu/directory/jared-christensen">
					accomplices</a>). I deeply respect my colleagues (especially 
					in the Department of Linguistics) and their research.</td>
				</tr>
			</table>
			<p style="line-height: 150%" align="justify"><br>
			The following are links to the
			<a target="_top" href="https://scholar.google.com/">Google Scholar</a> profiles for
			Professors and Associate Professors in the College of Humanities at
			<a href="https://www.byu.edu/">BYU</a>,
			who had a Google Scholar profile in October 2020. As can be
			seen, <b><a target="_top" href="https://www.mark-davies.org/">one person</a></b> had roughly <b>one third</b> of the <b>total citations</b> for
			the (Full) Professors and Associate Professors <b>in the entire college</b>. 
			This list was created at roughly the same time that Mark Davies 
			<a href="leaving-byu.asp">left BYU</a> in 2020.</p>


