			Chapman, Jeremy Browne, Ryan Christensen, Travis Anderson, Erik
			Larson, Gregory Stallings, Rob Smead, Willis Fails.<p style="line-height: 150%" align="justify">It is possible that some of these individuals had a
			number of citations to their research as well. Our experience, however, is that those
			faculty who would have the &quot;most robust&quot; Google Scholar profiles
			(i.e. who have published the most, and who have been cited the most) are
			the ones who actually bother to create one in the first place. For example, Linguistics is the department in the College of
			Humanities that (per capita) has the highest number of publications
			every year (2x-3x the college average), and it also has the highest percentage of faculty with
			Google Scholar profiles. </p>
			</td>
		</tr>
